From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenMyGlass <openmyglass@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 23:39:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] cpufreq: interactive: default input_boost to 1

Turn the touchscreen input boost on by default, so that the Glass
touchpad raises the CPUs to hispeed_freq on the first report of a swipe
or tap instead of waiting for the next timer sample (timer_rate, 20 ms)
to see the load.

On Glass the matching device is the Synaptics touchpad, driven by the
RMI4 F11 driver ("sensor00fn11"). It reports EV_ABS with
ABS_MT_POSITION_X/Y (multi-touch protocol A) and has no BTN_TOUCH,
ABS_X or ABS_Y, so it matches the first entry of the id table (the
"touchscreen" one) and not the "touchpad" one. No other input driver built for notle reports
the multi-touch axes (the light and proximity sensor uses ABS_MISC and
ABS_DISTANCE, the IMU is an IIO device). A USB or Bluetooth HID device,
or a uinput device, with those axes would match too.

The boost never goes above hispeed_freq, which is limited to the
policy's scaling_max_freq, so it uses no frequency or voltage that the
governor could not already select. Each report sets the floor to
hispeed_freq, so the speed is held there for min_sample_time (80 ms by
default) after the last report. Writing 0 to
/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/interactive/input_boost turns it off.

This change is licensed under GPL-2.0-only, like the file it changes.
---
 Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt  | 4 +++-
 drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c | 2 +-
 2 files changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt b/Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt
index 5998c1b..0c61b9f 100644
--- a/Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt
+++ b/Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt
@@ -277,7 +277,9 @@ on a write to boostpulse, before allowing speed to drop according to
 load as usual.  Default is 80000 uS.
 
 input_boost: If non-zero, boost speed of all CPUs to hispeed_freq on
-touchscreen activity.  Default is 0.
+touchscreen activity.  Default is 1.  The speed is held at
+hispeed_freq or above for min_sample_time after the last input report,
+then drops according to load as usual.
 
 
 3. The Governor Interface in the CPUfreq Core
diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c
index d778341..bf53b46 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c
@@ -112,7 +112,7 @@ static u64 boostpulse_endtime;
  * Boost pulse to hispeed on touchscreen input.
  */
 
-static int input_boost_val;
+static int input_boost_val = 1;
 
 struct cpufreq_interactive_inputopen {
 	struct input_handle *handle;
